---
description: Fan work out to worktree-isolated subagents so parallel edits never collide
argument-hint: "[the task to parallelize]"
---
Parallelize this work across isolated worktrees: $ARGUMENTS

## 1. Split into independent slices
Break the task into slices that touch **disjoint sets of files** and have no cross-dependencies.
If two slices would edit the same file, keep them in one slice (or sequence them). List the
slices before spawning anything.

## 2. Spawn one worktree-isolated subagent per slice
Delegate each slice to a subagent that runs in its **own git worktree** (use the `implementer`
role, or tell the agent to "use worktrees for your agents"). Each worktree lives under
`.claude/worktrees/<name>/` on its own branch, and Claude Code enforces the isolation — a
worktree subagent cannot edit the main checkout. Give each subagent only its slice's files.

Note: if Agent Teams is enabled, a *named* delegation becomes a shared-directory teammate, not an
isolated subagent. For isolated parallel *writes*, use this subagent/worktree path.

Each worker must end its final message with a line `Confidence: NN%` before finishing (a
`SubagentStop` hook can block a writer that isn't confident enough; only that line counts).

## 3. Consolidate and merge back
When the subagents finish, review their results, then **merge each worktree's branch** back into
your working branch (resolve conflicts if any), and **clean up**: `git worktree remove` each one.

## 4. Hygiene
Ensure `.claude/worktrees/` is git-ignored: `git check-ignore -q .claude/worktrees/x` succeeds (ocgen
ignores it locally while `.claude/` isn't committed); if it fails, add the line to `.gitignore`.
`.worktreeinclude` already copies local files like `.env`, and the uncommitted config, into each new
worktree.
